# Operator policy for Rover. The gateway reads this at start.
# Values are quoted: the tier bindings contain '|', which a shell would
# otherwise treat as a pipeline when this file is sourced.
#
# Two independent gates, and the split is the point:
#   ROBOT_MD_TOOL_ALLOWLIST  which tools exist at all. Anything absent is a
#                            signed DENY before any driver code runs.
#   ROBOT_MD_TOOL_MIN_TIER   which BEARER tiers may invoke each tool. Keys off
#                            the tool name (operator-controlled), not the
#                            envelope's self-declared `scope` (caller-controlled).
#
# `arm.*` is deliberately absent: this robot is a car. Asking Rover to go home
# gets a signed tool_allowlist refusal, not a 404 and not an error.
ROBOT_MD_TOOL_ALLOWLIST="drive.set,drive.stop,drive.envelope.open,drive.envelope.revoke,status.report"
#
# drive.stop and drive.envelope.revoke are pinned at `read` — the lowest tier
# this gateway can express. A stop that a privilege check can refuse is not a
# stop, and there is no failure mode in which declining to halt a moving vehicle
# is the safer outcome. Everyone who can reach this gateway at all can stop the
# car and withdraw its permission to move.
#
# drive.envelope.open sits at `actuate` because opening an envelope IS the
# approval — it is the act that grants a budget of physical motion — so it costs
# the same tier as moving.
ROBOT_MD_TOOL_MIN_TIER="drive.set:actuate|commission,drive.envelope.open:actuate|commission,drive.stop:read|actuate|commission,drive.envelope.revoke:read|actuate|commission,status.report:read|actuate|commission"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# REAL WHEELS — commented out on purpose. Uncomment to switch Rover from
# SimulatedDrive to the PCA9685, then: systemctl --user restart {name}-gateway
#
# Left off because this file's own rule is that a driver built by accident must
# not be able to move a real vehicle. Enabling it is the deliberate act, and it
# belongs AFTER the bench steps in the PCA9685 bring-up checklist (wheels OFF the ground first) — not before.
#
# What happens the moment it is enabled: constructing the driver writes NEUTRAL
# to both channels and holds it for 0.5 s to arm the ESC. That is the safe
# state, but "neutral" is only neutral if the trims below are right, so the
# first power-on happens with the WHEELS OFF THE GROUND.
#
# If the board is not answering at its address, the actuator refuses to
# construct and rover-gateway will NOT START — loudly, in `journalctl --user -u
# {name}-gateway`, rather than quietly pretending to drive. The runtime
# (:8003) and its /api/stop are a separate service and stay up regardless.
#
#OPENCASTOR_DRIVE=pca9685
#OPENCASTOR_DRIVE_I2C_BUS=1
#OPENCASTOR_DRIVE_I2C_ADDRESS=0x40
#OPENCASTOR_DRIVE_THROTTLE_CHANNEL=0
#OPENCASTOR_DRIVE_STEERING_CHANNEL=1
#
# Per-vehicle trims — measure these, do not trust the defaults. See steps 4-5
# of the bring-up: the PCA9685's "25 MHz" oscillator has percent-level
# tolerance and scales every pulse, so a part running fast turns a commanded
# 1500 us neutral into a slow crawl in REVERSE with nothing wrong in software.
#OPENCASTOR_DRIVE_OSCILLATOR_HZ=25000000
#OPENCASTOR_DRIVE_THROTTLE_NEUTRAL_US=1500
#OPENCASTOR_DRIVE_STEERING_NEUTRAL_US=1500
#OPENCASTOR_DRIVE_STEERING_SPAN_US=300
#OPENCASTOR_DRIVE_THROTTLE_INVERT=false
#
# Only if your ESC ignores reverse without a neutral handshake. Off by default
# because the handshake sleeps and this layer sits under a 50 ms deadman.
#OPENCASTOR_DRIVE_ESC_REVERSE_ARMING=false
#OPENCASTOR_DRIVE_ESC_DOUBLE_TAP_REVERSE=false
